WEBVTT

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:17.490
<v Ira Glass>Hanco runs a shop that sells Vietnamese sandwiches and bubble tea on a corner in Brooklyn. It's a neighborhood favorite, lots of tables, and sunlight streaming in through big windows, crowded most of the day. Not long ago, a customer brought in a menu from a shop that opened up just four blocks away to show to Hanco.
<v Ira Glass>Hanco在布鲁克林的一个街角开了一家店，卖越南三明治和珍珠奶茶。那是附近很受欢迎的一家店，有很多桌椅，大窗户透进阳光，大部分时间都很拥挤。不久前，一位顾客带了一份菜单给Hanco看，那是四条街外刚开的一家店的菜单。

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:19.360
<v Hanco>Yeah. When I saw this, I was really shocked.
<v Hanco>是的。当我看到这个的时候，我真的很震惊。

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:24.680
<v Ira Glass>OK, so here's your menu. Just read the list of sandwiches from top to bottom.
<v Ira Glass>好了，这是你的菜单。只要从上到下读一遍三明治的列表。

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:29.950
<v Hanco>Classic, grilled pork, grilled chicken, sardine, shredded chicken, tofu.
<v Hanco>经典款、烤猪肉、烤鸡肉、沙丁鱼、鸡丝、豆腐。

00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:32.100
<v Ira Glass>Now let's go to this other one and read.
<v Ira Glass>现在让我们看看另外那一家店，读一下。

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:38.620
<v Hanco>OK, classic, grilled pork, grilled chicken, shredded chicken, tofu.
<v Hanco>好了，经典款、烤猪肉、烤鸡肉、鸡丝、豆腐。

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:42.340
<v Ira Glass>Or, go to the list of bubble teas. Here's Hanco's menu.
<v Ira Glass>或者，看看珍珠奶茶的列表。这是Hanco的菜单。

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:48.080
<v Hanco>Original bubble tea, almond, taro, honeydew coconut, coffee, thai tea.
<v Hanco>原味珍珠奶茶、杏仁味、芋头味、哈密瓜椰子味、咖啡味、泰式奶茶。

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:50.100
<v Ira Glass>And the competitive, Henry's.
<v Ira Glass>还有竞争对手Henry's的。

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:55.440
<v Hanco>Original bubble tea, almond, taro, honeydew coconut, coffee, thai.
<v Hanco>原味珍珠奶茶、杏仁味、芋头味、哈密瓜椰子味、咖啡味、泰式。

00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:22.250
<v Ira Glass>Of the 42 sandwiches, salads, appetizers and drinks on Hanco's menu, all 42 are on Henry's in the exact same order, laid out in the same places on the menu posted $0.25 to $0.50 cheaper. And when you hold the two menu side by side, it's the same font, same colors, maroon letters on white. Each one says in cursive, "free delivery on the upper right". The two menus look like copies except for the name on top.
<v Ira Glass>在Hanco菜单上的42种三明治、沙拉、开胃菜和饮料中，这42种全都在Henry's的菜单上，顺序完全一样，在菜单上的位置也一样，价格还便宜了0.25到0.50美元。当你把两份菜单放在一起对比时，字体一样，颜色一样，白底栗色字。每一份菜单的右上角都用草书写着“免费外送”。这两份菜单看起来就像复印件，除了顶部的名字不同。

00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:28.950
<v Hanco>I guess they're being lazy. They don't want to be great creative. You're just like, "you know what, let's just copy and paste".
<v Hanco>我猜他们就是懒。他们不想发挥创意。他们只会想：“你知道吗，我们就复制粘贴吧。”

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:47.750
<v Ira Glass>The three people who owned this new place have all worked for Hanco at some point or another. There was Henry, there was Henry's mom, and there was Hui. Hanco says in retrospect, they were always asking, "how do you make this? What's the recipe for that?" And at the time he thought they were just trying to be helpful, until they opened up their own place and he put it all together.
<v Ira Glass>这家新店的三位老板都在Hanco工作过。有Henry，Henry的妈妈，还有Hui。Hanco回想起来说，他们总是问：“这个怎么做？那个的配方是什么？”当时他以为他们只是想帮忙，直到他们开了自己的店，他才把这一切联系起来。

00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:55.210
<v Ira Glass>For two nights after he saw the menu, Hanco couldn't sleep. He had trouble breathing. He wondered if he was having a heart attack. He was really upset.
<v Ira Glass>看到那份菜单后的两个晚上，Hanco彻夜难眠。他感到呼吸困难。他怀疑自己是不是心脏病发作了。他真的很沮丧。

00:01:55.210 --> 00:02:33.290
<v Hanco>I understand like you want to start your own thing. But you don't have to be identical. Do something different. I mean, even though if you get my recipe, you stole my recipe. Or you didn't steal it. You just know it. I teach you. You know it. You work here for many years, you know the recipe. You start your own business. You open across the street, the next block. I mean, that's your choice. I can't do anything about that. You know, that's how it is. Why do you do identical? Why do you confuse customers? Because customers come to me, "is the the same as Hanco's".
<v Hanco>我理解你想开创自己的事业。但你不必做得一模一样。做点不同的东西吧。我的意思是，即使你拿到了我的配方，你偷了我的配方。或者你没偷。你只是知道而已。我教过你。你在这里工作了很多年，你知道配方。你开始了自己的生意。你在街对面、隔壁街开店。我的意思是，那是你的选择。我对此无能为力。你知道，事实就是这样。为什么要做得一模一样？为什么要让顾客困惑呢？因为顾客跑来问我：“这和Hanco的一样吗？”

00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:45.540
<v Ira Glass>It has hurt Hanco's business. Sales are down 10% to 15% he said. Meanwhile up the street at Henry's, things are going so badly them Henry and his mom left after just a few months. Now Hui ran the place alone.
<v Ira Glass>这对Hanco的生意造成了损害。他说销售额下降了10%到15%。与此同时，在街上的Henry's，情况非常糟糕，Henry和他的妈妈只开了几个月就离开了。现在是Hui一个人在经营这家店。

00:02:45.540 --> 00:03:18.220
<v Ira Glass>So I marched up the street to see what Hui had to say about all of this. Because Vietnamese sandwiches and bubble tea, they're an idea whose time has come, and not just in Brooklyn. They're popping up all over the country. You you buy Vietnamese banh mi sandwiches in Biloxi, Mississippi right now. And bubble tea, you can get it in Idaho. With all that potential and opportunity out there, all that money to be made, why did Hui and Henry imitate this one restaurant four blocks away so slavishly all the way down to the font on the menu? What were they thinking?
<v Ira Glass>所以我一路走到那条街上，想看看Hui对这一切有什么说法。因为越南三明治和珍珠奶茶，它们是一个适逢其时的好点子，而且不仅仅是在布鲁克林。它们在全国各地如雨后春笋般涌现。你现在在密西西比州的比洛克西都能买到越南三明治。还有珍珠奶茶，在爱达荷州也能买到。有着如此大的潜力和机会，有着那么多可以赚的钱，为什么Hui和Henry要如此机械地模仿四条街外这家餐馆，甚至连菜单字体都照搬？他们在想什么？

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:37.800
<v Ira Glass>And when I got to Henry's to ask this-- it's this tiny little shop-- what happened next was very, very strange. There was a man and two women behind the counter. And they swore they had never heard of anyone, anyone, named Hui. "No, no," they said, "This place had a new owner, some guy in Queens name Joey".
<v Ira Glass>当我到Henry's问这个问题时——那是一家非常非常小的店——接下来发生的事情非常非常奇怪。柜台后面有一个男人和两个女人。他们发誓说从未听说过叫Hui的人。“不，不，”他们说，“这家店换了新老板，是个叫Joey的皇后区人。”

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:51.670
<v Ira Glass>Does anyone work here who used to work at Hanco's? I brought a Cantonese translator with me because I've been told the Hui's English wasn't so great. Hui is Chinese, not Vietnamese.
<v Ira Glass>有没有人在柜台工作过，以前在Hanco工作过？我带了一位广东话翻译，因为有人告诉我Hui的英语不是很好。Hui是华人，不是越南人。

00:03:51.670 --> 00:03:57.290
<v Hue>I used to deliver food at Hanco's.
<v Hue>我以前在Hanco送餐。

00:03:57.290 --> 00:03:58.840
<v Ira Glass>And what's your name?
<v Ira Glass>那你叫什么名字？

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:00.650
<v Ling>Me?
<v Ling>我？

00:04:00.650 --> 00:04:03.390
<v Ira Glass>The guy behind the counter got a funny look on his face.
<v Ira Glass>柜台后面的那个男人露出了一种奇怪的表情。

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:08.330
<v Ling>Ling, I'm Ling. Ling, L-I-N-G, Ling.
<v Ling>Ling，我叫Ling。Ling，L-I-N-G，Ling。

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:27.980
<v Ira Glass>Yes, Ling told me he had worked at this place since the very beginning, seven months before. But no, he had no idea who this Hui was. He had never heard that name. When I asked him if they knew anything about how they came up with a menu, if they stole from Hanco's, Ling would say things that seemed to indicate that he had no problem copying the menu.
<v Ira Glass>是的，Ling告诉我他从最开始就在这家店工作，七个月前就开始了。但他说他不知道Hui是谁。他从未听说过那个名字。当我问他们是否知道菜单是怎么来的，是否是从Hanco偷来的，Ling说的话似乎表明他并不介意复制菜单。

00:04:27.980 --> 00:04:37.070
<v Ling>If you're like a kung fu student, if the master didn't teach you, how are you supposed to learn?
<v Ling>如果你像个功夫学徒，如果师傅不教你，你该怎么学呢？

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:40.480
<v Ira Glass>And then in the very next, he would say.
<v Ira Glass>然后紧接着，他又会说。

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:53.510
<v Ling>But I won't copy them. We do their own thing. They do their own thing. There's a difference between the two restaurants and we just made a new menu. Take a look at it.
<v Ling>但我不会抄他们的。我们做我们自己的东西。他们做他们自己的东西。这两家餐馆有区别，我们刚刚做了新的菜单。看一看吧。

00:04:53.510 --> 00:05:11.580
<v Ira Glass>And, in fact, the new menu was glossy, and multi-colored, and didn't look like Hanco's menu at all. Though, of course, it still contained most of the items from Hanco's menu. "Stop talking about the past," Ling kept saying. "We don't know about the old menu and how it was made, we only know about no."
<v Ira Glass>事实上，新的菜单是光面的，多色的，看起来一点也不像Hanco的菜单。尽管当然，它仍然包含Hanco菜单上的大部分项目。“别再谈论过去的事了，”Ling一直说，“我们不知道旧菜单，也不知道它是怎么做出来的，我们只知道现在的。”

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:37.900
<v Ira Glass>Back out on the street, the translator, his name was Lee, and we couldn't figure out what just happened. Ling had told us that he thought it would be perfectly acceptable if they copied from Hanco. But then he also said that they didn't copy from Hanco. Also, somehow he had never heard of Hui . None of them had. Even though Ling had been at the restaurant since the day that Hui and Henry started it seven months before. None of it made any sense. And it was hard to let it go.
<v Ira Glass>回到街上，翻译Lee和我无法弄清楚刚才发生了什么。Ling告诉我们他认为如果他们复制Hanco的东西是完全可以接受的。但后来他又说他们没有复制Hanco。而且，不知何故他从未听说过Hui。他们都没有听说过。尽管Ling从Hui和Henry七个月前开店起就在那里工作了。这一切都说不通。而且很难就此放下。

00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:41.090
<v Lee>They don't know who Hui is apparently.
<v Lee>显然他们不知道Hui是谁。

00:05:41.090 --> 00:05:43.910
<v Ira Glass>But Ling worked here with Hui .
<v Ira Glass>但Ling和Hui一起在那里工作过。

00:05:43.910 --> 00:06:02.660
<v Lee>He didn't know the name Hui , but he knew the old owner, whoever the old owner was. But basically, I'm pretty sure he does know who Hui is by his body language. He wasn't answering. Other questions he was answering. He just with avoiding the question whenever I mention Hui .
<v Lee>他不知道Hui这个名字，但他认识以前的老板，不管以前的老板是谁。但基本上，根据他的肢体语言，我很确定他知道Hui是谁。他不回答。其他问题他都回答了。每当我提到Hui时，他就在回避这个问题。

00:06:02.660 --> 00:06:04.730
<v Ira Glass>Do you think he's Hui?
<v Ira Glass>你觉得他就是Hui吗？

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:17.270
<v Lee>I don't think so. But that would be funny if he was. He did take a while to answer his name his own name. So I don't know if that's really his name.
<v Lee>我不这么认为。但如果他是的话就太滑稽了。他确实过了好一会儿才回答出自己的名字。所以我不知道那是不是他真正的名字。

00:06:17.270 --> 00:06:20.620
<v Ira Glass>Let's take his picture.
<v Ira Glass>我们拍张照吧。

00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:31.480
<v Ira Glass>I pull out my digital camera, walk back into the restaurant, stand right in front of the counter facing Ling and the two other employees. I say nothing, point the camera. I shoot. I walk out.
<v Ira Glass>我拿出数码相机，走回店里，站在柜台前，面对着Ling和其他两名员工。我一言不发，对准相机。我按下了快门。然后走开了。

00:06:31.480 --> 00:06:39.440
<v Ira Glass>Four minutes later I'm back down the street inside the place where I started, the first restaurant, Hanco's. I show Hanco the photo and ask if he recognizes anybody.
<v Ira Glass>四分钟后，我回到了街上，进入了我开始的那家店，也就是Hanco的店。我给Hanco看照片，问他是否认识其中的任何人。

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:46.500
<v Hanco>That's Hui with the white hat. I've never seen this one. This is probably his wife.
<v Hanco>那个戴白帽子的是Hui。我没见过这个人。这个可能是他老婆。

00:06:46.500 --> 00:07:19.170
<v Ira Glass>So we had back four blocks in the other direction to Henry's store. And without the tape recorder on, I explained to Ling that I know he's actually Hui . And I told him that I'm not trying to embarrass him. But please could he just explain to me why he and Henry decided to knock off Hanco's menu so thoroughly and completely. He tells me, basically, that it was Henry's idea. Henry, when I check with him, says that it was actually Hui's idea. But neither of them could actually understand what the big deal was with copying a menu.
<v Ira Glass>所以我们朝另一个方向往回走了四条街回到Henry's的店。在录音机没开的情况下，我向Ling解释说我知道他其实就是Hui。我告诉他我不是想让他难堪。但请他解释一下为什么他和Henry决定如此彻底、完全地抄袭Hanco的菜单。他告诉我，基本上是Henry的主意。当我向Henry核实时，他说其实是Hui的主意。但他们两个都无法理解复制菜单有什么大不了的。

00:07:19.170 --> 00:07:37.790
<v Ira Glass>"All restaurants take from each other", Hui told me. You'll have two Japanese restaurants across the street from each other. They'll both sell the same items. That's how it always goes." In fact, Hanco, the guy with the first shop in the neighborhood, Hanco will tell anybody's who is curios that he got the idea for his place by visiting another Vietnamese sandwich shop in New York, one called Nicky's.
<v Ira Glass>“所有的餐馆都互相借鉴，”Hui告诉我，“你会看到街对面有两家日本餐馆。它们卖的东西都一样。事情总是这样的。”事实上，Hanco，那个在附近开第一家店的人，如果有人好奇，他会告诉任何人，他的开店灵感来自于参观纽约的另一家越南三明治店，那家店叫Nicky's。

00:07:37.790 --> 00:08:02.720
<v Ira Glass>Sandwiches and bubble tea are this unlikely million dollar idea. It's this unlikely idea, but it started at one job and it spread like a virus through Brooklyn, infecting one small, aspiring businessmen after another, leaving a trail of jealousies, and ambitions, and hurt feelings in its wake. None of these three guys are talking to each other anymore. Apparently a million dollar idea can do that, even if the idea does not make you $1 million.
<v Ira Glass>三明治和珍珠奶茶是一个令人意想不到的百万美元点子。这真是一个不可思议的点子，但它始于一份工作，然后像病毒一样在布鲁克林传播开来，感染了一个又一个雄心勃勃的小企业主，留下了嫉妒、野心和受伤的感情。这三个人现在都不再说话了。显然，一个百万美元的点子可以做到这一点，即使这个点子并没有真正让你赚到100万美元。

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:29.840
<v Ira Glass>Today on our radio show, we have stories of million dollar ideas, some of them with enough juice in them that they're actually worth actual millions, some of them only good enough to turn your life upside down and wreak havoc as you chase after them. At WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life distributed by Public Radio International. I'm Ira Glass, our show in four acts today. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>今天在我们的广播节目中，我们有关于百万美元点子的故事，其中一些充满了潜力，确实价值数百万，另一些则只会把你的生活搞得一团糟，让你在追逐它们时满地鸡毛。在WBEZ芝加哥，这是由公共广播国际发行的《美国生活》。我是Ira Glass，今天我们的节目分为四个篇章。请继续收听。

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:54.280
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Going Up? There's a whole world of engineers and investors who are actively trying to invent million dollar ideas. And in that world, there's something called an elevator pitch, which is pretty much exactly what you would guess it is. It's the pitch that you would make for your product, for your idea, if you bumped into a rich investor on an elevator. Figure you have 60 seconds, what's the vital stuff that will convince them. What are the details that you leave out?
<v Ira Glass>第一篇章，上去吗？有一整个工程师和投资者的世界，他们正在积极尝试发明百万美元的点子。在这个世界里，有一种东西叫“电梯演讲”，这和你猜的完全一样。这就是当你和一位富有投资者在电梯里偶遇时，你会为你的产品、你的点子所做的推销。想象你有60秒的时间，哪些是能说服他们的关键内容？哪些细节你会略过？

00:08:54.280 --> 00:09:09.310
<v Ira Glass>This is such a specific skill, apparently. MIT actually has a contest for its students and faculty with thousands of dollars in prices for elevator pitches. There's a little video on YouTube encourage people to pitch in this contest.
<v Ira Glass>显然这是一项非常具体的技能。麻省理工学院甚至为学生和教职员工举办了一场比赛，有数千美元的奖金奖励给电梯演讲。YouTube上有一个小视频，鼓励人们参加这个比赛。

00:09:09.310 --> 00:09:21.870
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "SEXY PITCH" BY  MIT $100K ENTREPRENEURSHIP COMPETITION]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 麻省理工学院10万美元创业竞赛的《性感演讲》]

00:09:21.870 --> 00:09:40.830
<v Ira Glass>Get it? Tim Rowe was a judge at this year's elevator pitch contest. He's an investor. And he runs what he describes as basically a big frat house, filled with start-up companies called the Cambridge Innovation Center. He told me, "Elevator pitches actually do happen in real life." He's even witnessed one in an elevator. But usually they go like.
<v Ira Glass>懂了吗？Tim Rowe是今年电梯演讲比赛的评委。他是一位投资者。他经营着他所描述的基本上是一个大型互助会，里面全是初创公司，叫剑桥创新中心。他告诉我：“电梯演讲确实发生在现实生活中。”他甚至在电梯里亲眼见过一次。但通常它们是这样的。

00:09:40.830 --> 00:10:01.700
<v Tim Rowe>You're walking through the halls at, say, MIT. And you see a professor there. And the professor is standing with a visitor. And the visitor turns out to be a famous venture capitalist. Right? And the professor turns to you and says oh, Joe, this is so and so. He's looking around for interesting things to invest in. And this is your moment, right?
<v Tim Rowe>你走在麻省理工学院的走廊里，看到一位教授站在那里。教授旁边有一位访客。访客原来是一位著名的风险投资家。对吧？教授转过身对你说：“哦，Joe，这位是某某某。他正在寻找有趣的项目进行投资。”这就是你的时刻了，对吧？

00:10:01.700 --> 00:10:04.810
<v Ira Glass>Wait, wait, wait, and you've actually seen this happen?
<v Ira Glass>等等，等等，等等，你真的见过这种情况发生吗？

00:10:04.810 --> 00:10:20.530
<v Tim Rowe>This happens literally every day in Cambridge, Massachusetts. There are probably 100 venture capitalist circling, looking for the next thing to invest in. And so it's these chance meetings, it happens regularly.
<v Tim Rowe>这在马萨诸塞州的剑桥确实每天都在发生。可能有100位风险投资家在这里徘徊，寻找下一个投资目标。所以这些偶遇是经常发生的。

00:10:20.530 --> 00:10:45.720
<v Ira Glass>The basics of a good elevator pitch, he says, are straightforward, talk in a way your grandparents could understand, don't expect the listener is an expert or knows scientific jargon. That's apparently a challenge for lots of engineers. You also have to explain how you and your team are qualified to actually accomplish the thing you're setting out to do. You have to prove you're an expert in field, if you've got Nobel Laureates working with you, it would be a good time to mention it. And finally, engage the listeners emotions. Grab them from the start.
<v Ira Glass>他说，一个好的电梯演讲的基本要素很简单，用你祖父母都能听懂的方式说话，不要指望听众是专家或懂科学术语。显然这对许多工程师来说是个挑战。你还必须解释你和你的团队有什么资质来完成你准备做的事情。你必须证明你是该领域的专家，如果你有诺贝尔奖得主和你一起工作，那是提到的好时机。最后，调动听众的情绪。从一开始就抓住他们。

