<v Kumail Nanjiani>接下来，用吸烟的方式换取财务健康。这将在片刻后播出。来自芝加哥公共广播和公共广播国际，我们的节目继续播出。

00:22:13.720 --> 00:22:36.970
<v Ira Glass>This American Life, I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our show, of course, we choose a theme, bring you different kinds of stories on that team. Today's show, million dollar idea, stories of great ones, and less successful ones. We have arrived it act three of our show, Act Three, Get Rich or Die Trying. Chana Joffe-Walt has the story of our next million dollar idea, a very, very ambitious idea.
<v Ira Glass>《美国生活》，我是Ira Glass。当然，每周我们的节目都会选择一个主题，为你带来关于这个主题的不同故事。今天的节目是“百万美元点子”，有关于伟大点子的故事，也有关于不那么成功点子的故事。我们已经到了节目的第三篇章，第三篇章，富起来或者在尝试中死去。Chana Joffe-Walt带来了我们下一个百万美元点子的故事，一个非常、非常雄心勃勃的点子。

00:22:36.970 --> 00:22:52.700
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>This particular million dollar idea was to take something generally seen as bad and turn it good, death. I think it's safe to say, universally seen as a bad thing. So take that and make people see good in it. That was the plan.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这个特定的百万美元点子是将通常被视为坏事的东西变成好事，死亡。我想可以肯定地说，死亡被普遍视为坏事。所以利用这一点，让人们看到其中的好处。这就是计划。

00:22:52.700 --> 00:23:17.650
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It started in Prague in 1999. A couple law makers begin unwittingly laying the groundwork for a million dollar idea, by doing something tedious, something lawmakers always do, talking about the budget, specifically talking about raising taxes on cigarettes to cover that budget. Czech reporters then began doing something tedious reporters always do, randomly asking smokers on the street for their two cents.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>它始于1999年的布拉格。几位立法者在不知不觉中开始为一个百万美元的点子奠定基础，他们做了一件乏味的事情，立法者总是做的事情，讨论预算，特别是讨论提高香烟税以覆盖预算。捷克记者随后开始做记者总是做的乏味事情，随机询问街上的吸烟者他们的看法。

00:23:17.650 --> 00:23:29.720
<v Prague Reporter>On a busy Prague street, some shrugged saying at most, they would cut down, while others admitted once prices rose, they would consider quitting for good.
<v Prague Reporter>在繁忙的布拉格街道上，有些人耸耸肩说最多他们会减少吸烟量，而另一些人承认一旦价格上涨，他们会考虑彻底戒烟。

00:23:29.720 --> 00:23:42.170
<v Prague Smoker  1>I guess I'm going to have to quit. What can you do? I'm a pension earner. And it really hits my pension check. I've already reduced the number I smoke to only ten a day.
<v Prague Smoker  1>我想我不得不戒烟。你能怎么办？我是领养老金的人。这真的会影响我的养老金支票。我已经把每天抽的数量减少到只有十支了。

00:23:42.170 --> 00:23:52.230
<v Prague Reporter>Definitely it's a good thing. I'm for it. When the prices go up, it will force me to consider whether I really want to put my money into cigarettes instead of, for example, sport.
<v Prague Reporter>这绝对是件好事。我支持。当价格上涨时，它会迫使我考虑我是否真的想把钱花在香烟上，而不是花在比如体育运动上。

00:23:52.230 --> 00:24:09.560
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>You know whose dog whistles that only dogs can here, this kind of man on the street news clip where people are casually throwing around the words quit, and put money somewhere else, is a tobacco executives dog whistle. They pay attention immediately. And that attention is undivided.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你知道只有狗能听到的那种狗哨吗？这种街头新闻片段里，人们随意抛出“戒烟”、“把钱花在别处”这些词，就是烟草高管的狗哨。他们会立即注意。而且那种注意力是全神贯注的。

00:24:09.560 --> 00:24:40.850
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Those smokers in 1999 more than likely smoked Phillip Morris cigarettes. The company was the largest manufacturer in the Czech Republic by far, and the largest in the world. And there was something specific about the Czech taxes conversation that got under the skin of Phillip Morris executives. It was this. Legislators were arguing that tax increases were necessary because smoking hurt the economy. People get sick from smoking. And in a place like the Czech Republic with public health care, the government has to pay for that.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>1999年的那些吸烟者很可能吸的是菲利普莫里斯（Philip Morris）香烟。该公司是捷克共和国遥遥领先的最大制造商，也是世界上最大的制造商。捷克的税收谈话中有些具体的事情触动了菲利普莫里斯高管的神经。就是这个。立法者辩称提高税收是必要的，因为吸烟损害经济。人们因吸烟而生病。而在像捷克共和国这样拥有公共医疗保健的国家，政府必须为此买单。

00:24:40.850 --> 00:24:55.110
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>This particular logic struck the Phillip Morris people as bizarre. Not just bizarre, wrong. And so inspired by accuracy and the threat of taxes, Phillip Morris came up with their million dollar idea.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这种特殊的逻辑让菲利普莫里斯的人感到离奇。不仅仅是离奇，而且是错误的。因此，受到准确性和税收威胁的启发，菲利普莫里斯想出了他们的百万美元点子。

00:24:55.110 --> 00:25:16.210
<v Gordan Fairclough>Well, they commissioned a study to tote up the costs and the benefits to the Czech government of its people's smoking habits. Gordan Fairclough covered tobacco for the Wall Street Journal. And he says, "In its simplest form, the idea was this, death, death saves you money, the more complicated form, a 28 page economic analysis.
<v Gordan Fairclough>嗯，他们委托了一项研究，来计算捷克政府国民吸烟习惯的成本和收益。《华尔街日报》报道烟草行业的Gordan Fairclough说：“最简单的形式，这个点子的核心是：死亡，死亡能为你省钱。”更复杂的形式是一份28页的经济分析报告。

00:25:16.210 --> 00:25:21.360
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Could you just read the executive summary of that study, just the bolded part?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你能读一下那份研究的执行摘要吗，就读加粗的部分？

00:25:21.360 --> 00:25:41.510
<v Gordan Fairclough>The bolded part, OK. So the beginning of the study says, "Based on up to date reliable data and consideration of all relevant contributing factors, the effect of smoking on the public finance balance in the Czech Republic in 1999 was positive, estimated at CZK 5.8 billion.
<v Gordan Fairclough>加粗的部分，好的。研究开头说：“基于最新的可靠数据和对所有相关因素的考量，1999年吸烟对捷克共和国公共财政平衡的影响是正面的，估计为58亿捷克克朗。”

00:25:41.510 --> 00:25:55.610
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Quick currency conversion interruption, CZK 5.8 billion in 2000, that's after $147 million a year, coming largely from tobacco-related taxes, but also from other things.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>快速货币换算中断，2000年的58亿捷克克朗，大约相当于1.47亿美元，主要来自烟草相关税收，但也来自其他方面。

00:25:55.610 --> 00:26:14.920
<v Gordan Fairclough>So basically, you know, what they're trying to do here is tote up here the gains and losses to public finances from smoking. So if you have people smoking and their life span is not as long, you record big savings on housing for the elderly, for example, [INTERPOSING VOICES].
<v Gordan Fairclough>所以基本上，你知道，他们在这里试图做的是统计吸烟对公共财政的收益和损失。所以如果人们吸烟，寿命不那么长，你就会记录下为老年人提供住房的大笔节省，例如，[重叠的声音]。

00:26:14.920 --> 00:26:16.790
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Because people die?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>因为人们死了？

00:26:16.790 --> 00:26:37.630
<v Gordan Fairclough>Because people die. Because people die early, you also pay less out in pensions and other social benefits to the elderly. People die early, you don't have to pay health care costs for all their non-smoking-related illnesses that they would get later in life, all of which in this study are seen on that kind of positive side of the ledger.
<v Gordan Fairclough>因为人们死了。因为人们早死，你在养老金和其他社会福利上支付给老人的钱也就少了。人们早死，你就不必为他们生命后期可能患上的所有非吸烟相关疾病支付医疗费用，所有这些在研究中都被视为账目的正面。

00:26:37.630 --> 00:27:04.350
<v Gordan Fairclough>On the negative side, you have the costs of fires started by people smoking, falling asleep in bed smoking, doing other things with cigarettes. The financial implications of people who stop working earlier, either because they die or they get too sick to work, so the state then loses the income tax that they might charge those people. Also on the cost side is, the cost of treating people for smoking-related diseases and for secondhand smoke.
<v Gordan Fairclough>在负面方面，你有因吸烟、在床上吸烟睡着、用香烟做其他事情引起火灾的成本。因为人们早死或太虚弱而无法工作，国家就会失去可能向这些人征收的所得税，这方面的财务影响也在负面。同样在成本方面的是，治疗人们的吸烟相关疾病和二手烟的成本。

00:27:04.350 --> 00:27:16.960
<v Gordan Fairclough>So the way these consultants tote that all up is people smoking is a net benefit, a net financial benefit to the Czech state of that CZK 5.8 billion.
<v Gordan Fairclough>所以顾问们统计这一切的方式是，吸烟对捷克政府来说是一个净收益，是一个价值58亿捷克克朗的净财务收益。

00:27:16.960 --> 00:27:37.110
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>The report is thorough. It begins by laying out its methodology, then later its quantification methods, concluding with a glossary that defines morbidity and mortality. Turns out-- who knew-- the technically uses of those two words are very different. And then five pages of references. Gordan Fairclough and I spent a long time reading through it together.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这份报告很详尽。它首先列出了方法论，然后是量化方法，最后以定义发病率和死亡率的词汇表结尾。事实证明——谁知道呢——这两个词的技术用法非常不同。然后还有五页的参考文献。Gordan Fairclough和我花了很多时间一起阅读它。

00:27:37.110 --> 00:27:57.500
<v Gordan Fairclough>So you don't have  to pay that. You don't have to pay the monthly insurance payments paid from the state budget for each pensioner, each retired person. Then you multiply that out by the 22,000 deaths due to tobacco smoking in the Czech Republic in 1999 with a nice reference to an epidemiology, one of the most famous of the tobacco controlled epidemiologists there.
<v Gordan Fairclough>所以你不必支付那个。你不必支付从国家预算中为每个养老金领取者，每个退休人员支付的每月保险金。然后你用这个乘以1999年捷克共和国因吸烟导致的22,000例死亡，并附带了流行病学方面的漂亮参考，那是那里最著名的烟草控制流行病学家之一。

00:27:57.500 --> 00:28:10.840
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So this is a weird thing. To get all these numbers to make their argument, Phillip Morris had to go to the only people who collect these numbers, their enemies, public health people, tobacco control lobbyists, basically anti-tobacco people.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>所以这是一件奇怪的事情。为了得到所有这些数字来支持他们的观点，菲利普莫里斯必须去寻找唯一收集这些数字的人，他们的敌人，公共卫生人员，烟草控制游说者，基本上就是反烟草人士。

00:28:10.840 --> 00:28:18.360
<v Gordan Fairclough>You know what the tobacco control people would tote up as the harm of smoking, and Phillip Morris is toting up as the benefit to the Czech state.
<v Gordan Fairclough>你知道烟草控制人员会把吸烟的危害加起来，而菲利普莫里斯却把它加起来作为捷克政府的收益。

00:28:18.360 --> 00:28:21.850
<v Matt Myers>I read the report and couldn't believe that they had put it in writing.
<v Matt Myers>我读了这份报告，简直不敢相信他们竟然把它写成了书面文件。

00:28:21.850 --> 00:28:46.980
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>This is one of the tobacco control people, Matt Myers. He works for the Campaign for Tobacco Free Kids in D.C. In the years leading up to the Phillip Morris study, Matt and a lot of other anti-tobacco people had done some research that told them to ease up on the smoking is bad for you message, and instead, focus on a message that seemed like it would be a lot more effective. Tobacco companies are bastards and they're out to get you.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这是烟草控制人员之一，Matt Myers。他在华盛顿为“无烟草青少年运动”工作。在菲利普莫里斯研究出炉前的几年里，Matt和其他许多反烟草人士做了一些研究，告诉他们减轻“吸烟有害健康”的信息，转而专注于一个看起来效率高得多的信息。烟草公司是混蛋，他们想害你。

00:28:46.980 --> 00:29:03.350
<v Matt Myers>For us, a report like this was truly like manna from heaven. This report was a complete gift. The next thing I did was sit down with our communications people and say what can we do with this?
<v Matt Myers>对我们来说，这样的报告简直就像天赐之物。这份报告完全是一份礼物。我做的下一件事就是和我们的公关人员坐下来谈论我们能用它做什么？

00:29:03.350 --> 00:29:34.970
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>The story ran in the Prague Post. Gordan wrote it up for the Wall Street Journal. It then quickly made it to CBS Evening News, ABC World News Tonight, in New York Times, and USA Today among many others. Some select newspaper headlines, "Death in the Ashes", "Tobacco Blows its Smoke" and "Smoking Cuts Elderly Costs and Elderly". Matt Myers and several other tobacco people ran there own ran their own ad campaign featuring a corpse at the morgue with a price tag dangling off a grey, big toe.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这个故事在《布拉格邮报》上刊登了。Gordan为《华尔街日报》写了它。随后它很快传到了CBS晚间新闻、ABC世界新闻、纽约时报和今日美国等媒体。一些精选的报纸头条包括，“灰烬中的死亡”、“烟草吹出的烟雾”和“吸烟减少了老年人的成本和老年人”。Matt Myers和其他几位反烟草人士发起了他们自己的广告活动，展示了一具停尸房里的尸体，一个大脚趾上挂着标价牌。

00:29:34.970 --> 00:29:43.170
<v Matt Myers>Let me put it this one, in one fell swoop, it allowed us to counter over $100 million of tobacco advertising. And that felt good.
<v Matt Myers>让我这样说，它一举让我们抵消了超过1亿美元的烟草广告宣传。这感觉很好。

00:29:43.170 --> 00:29:46.410
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Is was worth $100 million?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>它价值1亿美元吗？

00:29:46.410 --> 00:29:59.270
<v Matt Myers>Yes in critical respects. Getting our hands on that report allowed us to do with almost no funds, what the tobacco industry had spend $100 million trying to do.
<v Matt Myers>是的，在关键方面。得到这份报告让我们几乎不花什么资金，就做到了烟草行业花1亿美元试图做到的事情。

00:29:59.270 --> 00:30:21.570
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Phillip Morris apologized, then apologized again. Top executives repeated that they never authorized the study, that it was done by a Czech subsidiary of Phillip Morris without headquarter's permission. They issued public statements such as, "All of us at Philip Morris are extremely sorry. No one benefits from the very real, serious, and significant diseases caused by smoking".
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>菲利普莫里斯道歉了，然后又道歉了。高管们重申他们从未授权这项研究，它是菲利普莫里斯的捷克子公司在未经总部许可的情况下完成的。他们发表了公开声明，例如：“我们菲利普莫里斯的所有人都感到非常抱歉。吸烟引起的真实、严重且重大的疾病对任何人都没有好处。”

00:30:21.570 --> 00:30:29.070
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And their public relations guy in Prague, [UNINTELLIGIBLE] did one recorded interview, one poorly recorded interview with Czech Radio.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>而他们在布拉格的公关人员[听不清]在捷克电台做了一次录音采访，一次录音质量很差的采访。

00:30:29.070 --> 00:30:52.670
<v Phillip Morris Representative>We commissioned this study and it was not about health policy. But it was a part of an ongoing debate about the economics of cigarette excise tax policy in the Czech Republic. And I must say Phillip Morris deeply regrets any impression from this study that a premature death of smokers represents a benefit to this society.
<v Phillip Morris Representative>我们委托了这项研究，它与卫生政策无关。它是捷克共和国烟草消费税政策经济学争论的一部分。我必须说，菲利普莫里斯对这项研究可能产生的“吸烟者早死对社会有利”的印象深表遗憾。

00:30:52.670 --> 00:31:14.340
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>I talked with several Phillip Morris people from that time. Not one and them would allow me to record. So here's what I learned, In the midst of the media disaster, Phillip Morris set out to make sure this kind of thing never happen again. The company called a meeting in Switzerland, all the bosses from around the world came in, about 15 men, maybe a women or two were there. No one quite remembers.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我和当时菲利普莫里斯的几个人谈过。没有一个人允许我录音。所以我了解到的是，在媒体灾难中，菲利普莫里斯设法确保这种事情永远不会再发生。公司在瑞士召开了一次会议，来自世界各地的老板都来了，大约15个男人，也许有一两个女人在那里。没人记得很清楚。

00:31:14.340 --> 00:31:26.710
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And at that meeting, the guys from headquarters announced to the room, "You are not to talk about the death benefit, not in your reports, not in public, not ever again. We are not doing this anymore".
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>在那个会议上，总部的人向房间宣布：“你们不准谈论死亡收益，在你们的报告中不准谈，在公共场合不准谈，永远不要再谈。我们不再这样做这种事了。”

00:31:26.710 --> 00:32:02.010
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>The group listened to this for awhile. And then a single hand rose. "Excuse me, but they were the ones saying smoking costs the government more than it saves. Are we supposed to just let him lie?" Then there were more hands. "Yeah, what do you mean"? More than one person piped up with, "You're asking us to argue with one arm tied behind our back". And most popular of all in that room on that day, "But it's true. What we're saying is true. Early death does save society money".
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这群人听了一会儿。然后一只手举了起来。“抱歉，但正是他们说吸烟对政府的成本大于收益。我们难道就该让他们撒谎吗？”然后有更多举手。“是的，你是什么意思？”不止一个人插嘴说：“你这是要求我们自断一臂地去争论。”那天在房间里最受欢迎的话是：“但这是事实。我们所说的是真的。早死确实为社会省钱。”

00:32:02.010 --> 00:32:13.080
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Gordan Fairclough, a reporter with the Wall Street Journal would go to Phillip Morris headquarters during this time for interviews. And he says people really did seem incredulous at the negative response.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>《华尔街日报》的记者Gordan Fairclough在此期间前往菲利普莫里斯总部进行采访。他说人们看起来确实对负面反应感到难以置信。

00:32:13.080 --> 00:32:42.780
<v Gordan Fairclough>I think they become kind of hardened to this. There's a certain psychological distancing that goes on by tobacco executives, doing that work and selling this product knowing that it is, in fact, addictive, and knowing that it has such serious health consequences. And I think that some of that allows you to look at a report like this and not think to the average member of the general public this is going to seem incredibly callous.
<v Gordan Fairclough>我认为他们对这种事变得麻木了。烟草高管进行着一种心理疏离，做着那种工作，销售这种产品，明知它实际上是成瘾的，明知它有如此严重的健康后果。我认为其中一些让你能够看着像这样的报告，而不去想对于普通公众来说，这看起来会是多么冷酷无情。

00:32:42.780 --> 00:33:00.660
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It must be so strange for them too because there was such a long period of time where they were denying that smoking killed people. And now finally they're saying we're acknowledging it. We're writing the death benefit into our equations and you guys are still giving us crap.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>对他们来说肯定也很奇怪，因为有很长一段时间他们在否认吸烟会导致人们死亡。现在他们终于承认了。我们在我们的方程式中写下了“死亡收益”，你们这些家伙还在给我们找麻烦。

00:33:00.660 --> 00:33:12.060
<v Gordan Fairclough>That's true. They must have thought that was fairly ironic that, in fact, here they are admitting that this will end everyone's life earlier, and then they get slammed for it.
<v Gordan Fairclough>那是真的。他们肯定认为这很讽刺，事实上，他们在这里承认这会缩短每个人的生命，然后他们却因此被猛烈抨击。

00:33:12.060 --> 00:33:35.780
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>But after that, the tobacco people shut up about the death benefit, they fell in line, and they never publicly mentioned it again. The anti-tobacco people, though kind of won't let this go. They could have called attention to the study, declared outrage, mocked it, all of which they did do. And then they could have named the whole incident a success and walked away, which weirdly they did not do.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>但那之后，烟草界的人关于死亡收益闭嘴了，他们保持一致，再也没有公开提及。然而反烟草人士有点不依不饶。他们本可以引起对这项研究的注意，宣布愤怒，嘲笑它，所有这些他们都做了。然后他们本可以宣布整个事件是胜利并走开，这奇怪的是他们没有这样做。

00:33:35.780 --> 00:33:54.090
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Instead anti-tobacco people have spent the last decade trying to prove that the data, not just the moral question of counting deaths on the plus side of an economic analysis, but the actual data Phillip Morris is using was wrong, which when it comes to this study, seems a little bit besides the point. Doesn't it?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>相反，反烟草人士在过去十年里一直在努力证明数据是错误的，不仅仅是计算经济分析中死亡人数的道德问题，而是菲利普莫里斯使用的实际数据是错误的。这对于这项研究来说，似乎有点偏离重点了，不是吗？

00:33:54.090 --> 00:34:13.890
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>These guys clearly won this round, clear victory for tobacco control people, battle won. But they won't drop it. Instead, Matt Myers, with the Campaign for Tobacco Free Kids, has been doing his own studies about whether or not smokers save the government money. And his number show they do not.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这些人显然赢了这一轮，反烟草人士取得了明显的胜利，战斗获胜。但他们不愿放弃。相反，Matt Myers和“无烟草青少年运动”一直在做他们自己的研究，关于吸烟者是否为政府省钱。而他的数字显示并没有。

00:34:13.890 --> 00:34:23.770
<v Matt Myers>What we thought the data showed, and what it turned out that the data showed, was that tobacco was an enormous net drain on the economy. I think the numbers are very wrong.
<v Matt Myers>我们认为数据表明的是，数据最后表明，烟草对经济造成了巨大的净消耗。我认为那些数字是非常错误的。

00:34:23.770 --> 00:34:38.390
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>But it seems like part of the power of your position here is that you get to say look at those guys measuring the value of your death and the benefit of your death, that's ridiculous. And that's it. But then you are too. You're also doing your own economic analysis.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>但似乎你这个职位的力量部分在于你可以说“看看那些测量你死亡价值和你死亡收益的家伙，那太荒谬了”。就是这样。但后来你也是如此。你也在做你自己的经济分析。

00:34:38.390 --> 00:34:51.030
<v Matt Myers>We're very clear. Efforts to reduce tobacco use have nothing to do with economic costs. They have everything to do with quality of life.
<v Matt Myers>我们非常清楚。减少烟草使用的努力与经济成本无关。它们完全与生活质量有关。

00:34:51.030 --> 00:34:56.730
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>But you are doing economic analyses. You're doing cost-benefit studies yourself.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>但你在做经济分析。你自己就在做成本效益研究。

00:34:56.730 --> 00:35:15.380
<v Matt Myers>We did not start out making that argument. The public health community began to look at the economic issues only because the tobacco industry argued that as a society, we couldn't afford to reduce tobacco use. We have to respond to the tobacco industry's argument.
<v Matt Myers>我们一开始并没有提出那个论点。公共卫生界开始关注经济问题，仅仅是因为烟草行业声称作为一个社会，我们负担不起减少烟草使用。我们必须回应烟草行业的论点。

00:35:15.380 --> 00:35:36.610
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>In other words, they started it. And now it is on. So here we go, were they right? Does the fact that smokers die early mean that they save the rest of as money? Now Phillip Morris is obviously not the place you would go for an answer to that question. But luckily it is a question economists ask all the time about smoking and alcoholic obesity.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>换句话说，是他们先挑起的。现在已经开打了。所以我们开始吧，他们对了吗？吸烟者早死意味着他们为我们其他人省钱了吗？现在菲利普莫里斯显然不是你去寻找那个问题答案的地方。但幸运的是，这是一个经济学家一直在询问关于吸烟、酗酒和肥胖的问题。

00:35:36.610 --> 00:36:12.060
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And the way they do it is they go through all the costs to society. Payment for lung cancer treatment, that means everyone's insurance rates go up, more sic days. Subtract the savings to society, pensions aren't paid out plus spent on nursing home care because people die sooner. And they total it all up. And eventually most economists come to this unsatisfying conclusion. It about evens out. Smokers probably cost society a little bit more than they save by dying early, but only a little. And they more than make up for that cost by paying taxes on their smoke.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>他们这样做的方法是浏览社会的所有成本。支付肺癌治疗费用，这意味着每个人的保险费率都上涨，更多的病假。减去对社会的节省，因为人们死得早，养老金不支付，加上在疗养院护理上的花费也减少了。他们统计这一切。最终大多数经济学家得出了这个令人不满的结论。这大约抵消了。吸烟者通过早死可能比他们省下的钱多花社会一点钱，但只是多一点。而他们通过支付香烟税弥补了那部分成本。

00:36:12.060 --> 00:36:37.360
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>People like Matt Myers have one mean beef with this approach. It doesn't count the cost to the smoker. The fact that the smoker will probably lose four years of his life, it doesn't count that. Tobacco control people want to contact that. They do count that. And when they do, they come to the conclusion tobacco costs society greatly, a conclusion they talk about in advertising and lobbying all the time now.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>像Matt Myers这样的人对这种方法有一个主要的意见。它没有计算吸烟者的成本。吸烟者可能会损失四年生命的事实，它没有计算那个。烟草控制人员想要计算那个。他们确实计算了那个。当他们计算时，他们得出了烟草给社会造成了巨大成本的结论，这个结论他们现在一直在广告和游说中谈论。

00:36:37.360 --> 00:37:03.220
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And on the other side, Phillip Morris is completely quiet on this front. They don't point out that the majority of economists don't agree with that conclusion. They don't publish their own counter studies. And they don't talk about the death benefit, at least not publicly. They are quiet and watch their million dollar idea continue to live, just not on their side of the balance sheet.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>而在另一边，菲利普莫里斯在这方面完全安静。他们没有指出大多数经济学家不同意那个结论。他们没有发布他们自己的反向研究。他们也没有谈论死亡收益，至少不在公开场合。他们保持安静，看着他们的百万美元点子继续存在，只是不在他们的资产负债表那一侧。

00:37:03.220 --> 00:37:21.040
<v Ira Glass>NPR's Chana Joffe-Walt is part of the Planet Money team. Planet Money is a co-production of NPR News and our radio show. They bring you economic stories that are not boring on their blog and their free twice-weekly podcast at npr.org/money.
<v Ira Glass>NPR的Chana Joffe-Walt是“Planet Money”团队的一员。《Planet Money》是NPR新闻和我们广播节目的联合制作。他们在他们的博客和免费的每周两次的播客npr.org/money上为你带来不枯燥的经济故事。

00:37:21.040 --> 00:38:11.040
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "WINNERS NEVER QUIT" BY PEDRO THE LION]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - Pedro The Lion的《Winners Never Quit》]

00:38:11.040 --> 00:38:29.090
<v Ira Glass>Act Four, Don't hate the player. Somehow we've gotten this far into the show without anybody trying to make a fortune with an old fashioned scam, or out betting the casino, or beating the odds in some impossible way. Well let's correct that right this second with the story from Shawn Allee.
<v Ira Glass>第四篇章，别恨玩家。不知怎么的，我们在这个节目里走了这么远，居然没有人试图通过老式的骗局、或者打败赌场、或者以某种不可能的方式胜出而发大财。好吧，让我们现在就用Shawn Allee的故事纠正一下。

00:38:29.090 --> 00:38:43.580
<v Shawn Allee>In 1984, Michael Larson appeared on a CBS game show called Press Your Luck. He was just kind of contestant producers liked. He wore a simple grey suit, nothing flashing, and he had this aw shucks kind of smile like some guy from Ohio, when he was.
<v Shawn Allee>1984年，Michael Larson参加了CBS的一个游戏节目，叫《Press Your Luck》。他只是那种节目制作人喜欢的选手。他穿着简单的灰西装，没什么花哨的，他有着那种来自俄亥俄州的“哎呀抱歉”式的微笑，而他也确实来自那里。

00:38:43.580 --> 00:38:52.290
<v Game Show Host>Let's meet our second player, Michael Larson. How are you, Michael? Not Michael, what do you do for a living?
<v Game Show Host>让我们见见我们的第二位选手，Michael Larson。你好吗，Michael？不是Michael，你以什么为生？

00:38:52.290 --> 00:38:57.070
<v Michael Larson>Oh, I drive an ice cream truck in the summer. I hope to win enough money here not to do that this summer.
<v Michael Larson>哦，我夏天开冰淇淋车。我希望在这里赢足够的钱，这样夏天就不必那样做了。

00:38:57.070 --> 00:38:58.650
<v Game Show Host>Do you have it with you today?
<v Game Show Host>你今天带它来了吗？

00:38:58.650 --> 00:39:10.390
<v Ira Glass>What producers that day didn't know, is that Michael Larson had come on to Press Your Luck with a plan, a kind of far-fetched plan, a take 'em to the cleaners, a plan that was months in the making, a plan to get rich in half an hour.
<v Ira Glass>那天制作人不知道的是，Michael Larson带着一个计划来到了《Press Your Luck》，一个有点牵强的计划，一个“把他们清理干净”的计划，一个酝酿了几个月的计划，一个在半小时内发财的计划。

00:39:10.390 --> 00:39:13.590
<v Game Show Host>$56,851 and he's going again.
<v Game Show Host>56,851美元，他还要继续。

00:39:13.590 --> 00:39:14.400
<v Michael Larson>Stop
<v Michael Larson>停。

00:39:14.400 --> 00:39:16.130
<v Game Show Host>Stop at $5,000.
<v Game Show Host>停在5000美元。

00:39:16.130 --> 00:39:17.970
<v Game Show Host>[APPLAUSE]
<v Game Show Host>[掌声]

00:39:17.970 --> 00:39:38.920
<v Ira Glass>All his life, Michael was always running some little scam or another. Now Michael died in 1999. So you're not going to hear about any this from him. But I talked to his brother, James. Michael was the youngest of four boys. James was the oldest. James says even in middle school, Michael got caught smuggling candy bars into class and jacking the prices way above retail.
<v Ira Glass>他这一生，Michael总是搞些这样那样的骗局。Michael死于1999年。所以你不会从他那里听到这些。但我跟他弟弟James聊过。Michael是四个男孩中最小的。James是老大。James说即使在中学，Michael就被发现把糖果偷偷带进教室，并把价格抬高到零售价以上。

00:39:38.920 --> 00:39:47.080
<v Ira Glass>And James says after high school, though Michael was smart enough to go to college, he spent his time looking for easy ways to score.
<v Ira Glass>James说高中毕业后，虽然Michael聪明得足以去上大学，但他把时间花在寻找赚钱的捷径上。

00:39:47.080 --> 00:40:00.940
<v James Larson>He didn't understand the value of good, honest hard work, and just go, and work, and do your job, and get your pay. He thought those people were fools.
<v James Larson>他不懂那种诚实劳动、工作、做好本职工作并拿到报酬的价值。他认为那些人是傻瓜。

00:40:00.940 --> 00:40:23.190
<v Ira Glass>Michael's schemes ran the gamut. He started a business under a family member's name and then hired himself as an employee. Then he laid himself off to collect unemployment benefits. He'd watch for banks that would give you $500 for opening an account. He'd open one and wait the minimum time to collect the $500. Then he'd withdraw the money and do it again under a different name.
<v Ira Glass>Michael的计划五花八门。他以家人的名义开了一家公司，然后雇佣自己为员工。然后他解雇了自己以领取失业救济金。他会留意那些开户就送500美元的银行。他会开一个，等待领取500美元的最短时间。然后他会取走钱，用另一个名字再做一次。

00:40:23.190 --> 00:40:32.990
<v Ira Glass>His house was filled with stacks of newspapers everywhere, and boxes, and piles, which he'd scour for possible scams. He never threw anything out. He might need them something, he'd say.
<v Ira Glass>他的房子里到处堆满了报纸、盒子和杂物，他会为了可能的骗局搜寻这些。他从不扔掉任何东西。他可能会需要它们，他会说。

00:40:32.990 --> 00:40:50.640
<v James Larson>He used to sit at night and watch the TV and tape all these shows that you see on late night TV. You can make thousands of dollars by doing this or doing that. And he bought into that very much so.
<v James Larson>他过去常坐在晚上看电视，录下所有你在深夜电视上看到的节目。通过做这个或做那个，你可以赚几千美元。他非常相信这一点。

00:40:50.640 --> 00:40:58.390
<v Teresa Bertram>He had a TV in the bathroom. He had one in the bedroom. He had one in the kitchen. He would just carry them in.
<v Teresa Bertram>他在浴室里有一台电视。他在卧室里有一台。他在厨房里有一台。他会把它们搬进去。

00:40:58.390 --> 00:41:01.560
<v Ira Glass>This is Michael's former common law wife, Teresa Bertram .
<v Ira Glass>这是Michael的前事实婚姻伴侣Teresa Bertram。

00:41:01.560 --> 00:41:24.850
<v Teresa Bertram>He had like 12 in the living room lined up on the walls. They were the old consoles. And he would have one sitting on top of it and the other one sitting on top. And we had an entire wall full of 19 inch, 25 inch televisions. And he would watch them all at once. It got so hot back here it peeled the paint off the wall.
<v Teresa Bertram>他客厅的墙上排着12台电视。那是老式的控制台电视。他会在上面放一台，又在上面放另一台。我们整面墙全是19英寸、25英寸的电视。他会同时看它们。后面变得非常热，墙上的油漆都剥落了。

00:41:24.850 --> 00:41:46.400
<v Ira Glass>Teresa says she had a tough time explaining this TV business to visitors. Usually she'd just tell people Michael was straight up crazy. But Teresa says he was actually methodical with his TV viewing. He'd tune each TV to a different channel. The idea was to watch for anything that would make him rich. He's also watch TV ads, infomercials, and of course, game shows.
<v Ira Glass>Teresa说她很难向访客解释这事。通常她只会告诉人们Michael疯了。但Teresa说他在看电视方面其实很有方法。他会把每台电视调到不同的频道。点子是寻找任何能让他发财的东西。他还看电视广告、信息购物节目，当然还有游戏节目。

00:41:46.400 --> 00:41:52.830
<v Teresa Bertram>He was intrigued with the game shows. And he wanted to find one that he could do.
<v Teresa Bertram>他对游戏节目很感兴趣。他想找一个他能参与的。

00:41:52.830 --> 00:42:09.200
<v Ira Glass>Michael was convinced that someone smart could find loopholes in game shows that average people missed. So he'd videotape them all day and play them back in the evening. Teresa says sometimes he'd stay up all night fast-forwarding, rewinding, pausing, and re-watching, especially Press Your Luck.
<v Ira Glass>Michael确信聪明人可以在游戏节目中找到普通人错过的漏洞。所以他整天录像，晚上回放。Teresa说有时他会整晚熬夜，快进、倒带、暂停，然后重看，尤其是《Press Your Luck》。

00:42:09.200 --> 00:42:21.690
<v Teresa Bertram>I remember I was in the kitchen and he yelled for me to come into the living room. And he was real happy and excited. And he said, I've got it. I've figured it out.
<v Teresa Bertram>我记得那时我在厨房，他大喊让我去客厅。他非常开心和兴奋。他说，我抓住了。我弄明白了。

00:42:21.690 --> 00:42:33.070
<v Ira Glass>To understand what Michael was so excited about, you have to understand how Press Your Luck worked. If you don't know the show or remember it, maybe you've heard its catch phrase, big bucks, big bucks, no whammies.
<v Ira Glass>要理解Michael为什么这么兴奋，你必须了解《Press Your Luck》是如何工作的。如果你不知道这个节目或不记得它，也许你听过它的口号：“Big bucks, big bucks, no whammies.”（大奖大奖，没踩雷）。

00:42:33.070 --> 00:43:02.210
<v Ira Glass>Three contestants would take turns at the show's big game board. It was like a huge Monopoly board with squares around the edge. When a player took a turn, a light would bounce all over from square to square in no particular order, until a player would press a big red button and the light would stop. If they were lucky, the square they landed on would have a prize like a vacation trip or money. But if they weren't lucky, they'd hit a whammy. And they'd lost all the prizes, the money, everything.
<v Ira Glass>三名选手轮流操作节目的大型游戏板。它就像一个巨大的“大富翁”游戏板，四周都有方块。当玩家轮到时，灯光会在方块之间无序跳动，直到玩家按下大红按钮，灯光就会停止。如果幸运的话，他们停下的方块会有奖品，比如度假旅行或钱。但如果不幸运的话，他们会踩到“雷”（Whammy）。他们就会失去所有奖品、钱，一切。

00:43:02.210 --> 00:43:21.150
<v Ira Glass>All of the sounds simple enough. But here's the thing. To you, to me, and to the rest of America, the light on the Press Your Luck game board moved randomly. Michael knew we were wrong. Michael figured out that really there was nothing random about that game board. The light always moved in one of five patterns.
<v Ira Glass>听起来很简单。但事情是这样的。对你，对我，和对其他美国人来说，游戏板上的灯光移动是随机的。Michael知道我们错了。Michael算出游戏板上其实没什么随机的。灯光总是遵循五种模式之一。

00:43:21.150 --> 00:43:43.690
<v Teresa Bertram>He was memorizing the patterns. He wrote some stuff down. He took little notes. And then he started doing the VCR and hitting the pause button. He would show me in here because he got really excited then. Watch this. I can stop it every time where I want to.
<v Teresa Bertram>他记住了这些模式。他写下了一些东西。他记了点小笔记。然后他开始用录像机，按下暂停键。他会在里面给我展示，因为那时他真的非常兴奋。看这个。我可以每次都在我想要的地方停下来。

00:43:43.690 --> 00:43:53.340
<v Ira Glass>Michael spent months learning to push his VCR pause button at just the right moment, always on a prize, never on a whammy. Again, here's his brother, James.
<v Ira Glass>Michael花了几个月时间学习在准确的时机按下录像机暂停键，总是停在奖品上，从不踩雷。再说一次，这是他弟弟James。

00:43:53.340 --> 00:44:11.880
<v James Larson>When Michael told me he was going to go to California, I didn't think he'd had a chance. I thought he'd get out there and find that it's not as easy as you think it is. And so I said good luck. But don't call me for bus money to get back.
<v James Larson>当Michael告诉我他要去加州时，我不认为他有机会。我以为他到了那里会发现这并不像你想象的那么容易。所以我说祝好运。但别打电话给我要公交车费回来。

00:44:11.880 --> 00:44:14.320
<v Ira Glass>Which brings us back to the show.
<v Ira Glass>这把我们带回了节目。

00:44:14.320 --> 00:44:20.410
<v Game Show Host>Michael Larson. How are you, Michael? Now Michael, what do you do for a living?
<v Game Show Host>Michael Larson。你好吗，Michael？不是Michael，你以什么为生？

00:44:20.410 --> 00:44:22.740
<v Michael Larson>Oh, I drive an ice cream truck in the summer.
<v Michael Larson>哦，我夏天开冰淇淋车。

00:44:22.740 --> 00:44:33.980
<v Ira Glass>Michael took his seat on Press Your Luck on May 19, 1984. Watching the tape, his grey hair is slicked back receding a bit. His beard is halfway to grey. He looks nervous.
<v Ira Glass>Michael在1984年5月19日坐上了《Press Your Luck》的席位。看着录像，他的灰发向后梳，发际线有点退后。他的胡子花白了一半。他看起来很紧张。

00:44:33.980 --> 00:44:39.550
<v Game Show Host>Well, Michael, you want to earn enough money so you don't have to deal with your ice cream truck. You just want to eat the ice cream though.
<v Game Show Host>嗯，Michael，你想赚足够的钱，这样就不必应付你的冰淇淋车了。你只是想吃冰淇淋对吧？

00:44:39.550 --> 00:44:41.980
<v Michael Larson>No, I've done enough of that, too.
<v Michael Larson>不，那我也吃够了。

00:44:41.980 --> 00:44:46.190
<v Game Show Host>You've kind of OD'ed on ice cream, right? Well hopefully you won't OD on money, Michael.
<v Game Show Host>你吃冰淇淋吃得过量了，对吧？希望你不会因为钱过量，Michael。

00:44:46.190 --> 00:44:50.040
<v Darlene Lieblich Tipton>It was for me a very, very routine day.
<v Darlene Lieblich Tipton>对我来说，这是非常、非常平常的一天。

00:44:50.040 --> 00:44:58.240
<v Ira Glass>Darlene Lieblich Tipton was in the control room with the director and producers. Her job was to monitor the game and make sure everything was fair and square.
<v Ira Glass>Darlene Lieblich Tipton在控制室和导演、制片人在一起。她的工作是监控游戏，确保一切公平合理。

00:44:58.240 --> 00:45:08.500
<v Darlene Lieblich Tipton>I am looking at the same monitor that the director is looking at. So I see all the camera angles, all the shots. It's actually the best seat in the house.
<v Darlene Lieblich Tipton>我在看导演正在看的同一个监视器。所以我看到了所有的摄像角度，所有的镜头。这实际上是全场最好的座位。

00:45:08.500 --> 00:45:11.750
<v Ira Glass>Darlene says at first, Michael Larson didn't look like much.
<v Ira Glass>Darlene说起初，Michael Larson看起来并不怎么样。

00:45:11.750 --> 00:45:14.350
<v Michael Larson>I'm going, stop.
<v Michael Larson>我要说，停。

00:45:14.350 --> 00:45:17.880
<v Game Show Host>He got a whammy.
<v Game Show Host>他中雷了。

00:45:17.880 --> 00:45:36.330
<v Ira Glass>Right here, after getting a whammy on his first spin, Michael shakes. his head, grits his teeth, and forces a smile. He looks unsure of himself. On his next turn, he concentrates before hitting the red button, and lands on $4,000 dollars and extra turn. Sure he smiles. But more than anything, he looks relieved.
<v Ira Glass>就在这里，在他的第一次旋转中踩雷后，Michael摇了摇头，咬牙切齿，挤出一丝笑容。他看起来对自己没有信心。在他的下一次旋转时，他在按下红色按钮前进行了专注，停在4000美元和额外旋转上。当然，他笑了。但比起其他，他看起来更像是松了一口气。

00:45:36.330 --> 00:45:55.620
<v Ira Glass>After that he gets more confident. Turn after turn, he wracks up more and more money. By 18 grand, he reels back in his chair. At 21 grand, he waves his fists above his head. By 28 grand, Michael is literally bouncing in his seat. He's on a roll. And Michael, the host, the audience, everybody, they're all getting into it.
<v Ira Glass>在那之后，他变得更自信了。轮次过后，他赢了越来越多的钱。到1万8时，他向后倒在椅子上。到2万1时，他在头上挥舞拳头。到2万8时，Michael在座位上蹦蹦跳跳。他势不可挡。Michael、主持人、观众，每个人，他们都被带动起来了。

00:45:55.620 --> 00:46:27.580
<v Darlene Lieblich Tipton>It wasn't unusual for contestants to go on streaks. It was kind of the way the game was designed. After about, oh, I don't know, five to ten spins of the board, that's when it started to become obviously to people that he was hitting the same prize in the same square just about every time. And that skill, that's not random, and it's not luck.
<v Darlene Lieblich Tipton>选手出现连续获胜的情况并不罕见。这是游戏设计的方式。在大约，哦，我不知道，五到十次转板后，人们开始明显感觉到他几乎每次都停在同一个方块的同一个奖品上。那种技巧，那不是随机的，也不是运气的。

00:46:27.580 --> 00:46:28.570
<v Michael Larson>Stop.
<v Michael Larson>停。

00:46:28.570 --> 00:46:34.930
<v Michael Larson>[APPLAUSE]
<v Michael Larson>[掌声]

00:46:34.930 --> 00:46:57.400
<v Darlene Lieblich Tipton>He could aim and hit, which we didn't think was possible. And he continued to do it. Nobody else had ever done it that way. Everybody else had played it virtually like a slot machine. You just hit the button and it stops where it stops. He had it down to a skill, a fine art.
<v Darlene Lieblich Tipton>他可以瞄准并击中，这是我们认为不可能的。而且他继续这样做。以前没有人那样做过。每个人都是把它当作老虎机来玩的。你只是按下按钮，它在它停的地方停下。他把它练成了一种技巧，一种精湛的艺术。

00:46:57.400 --> 00:46:57.530
<v Michael Larson>Stop.
<v Michael Larson>停。

00:46:57.530 --> 00:47:00.030
<v Game Show Host>at $4,000 and a spin.
<v Game Show Host>停在4000美元和一个旋转上。

00:47:00.030 --> 00:47:24.560
<v Darlene Lieblich Tipton>I've been in the booth for thousands of episodes of game shows, never one like that. First the booth got very quiet. And then there was an oh my god, oh my god, oh my god. It was what do we do? What do we do? People were turning to me saying can we stop this?
<v Darlene Lieblich Tipton>我在演播室看过成千上万集游戏节目，从没见过像那样的。首先演播室变得非常安静。然后是一阵“我的天，我的天，我的天”。当时就是我们该怎么办？我们该怎么办？人们转向我说，我们能停止这个吗？

00:47:24.560 --> 00:47:46.320
<v Darlene Lieblich Tipton>He wasn't breaking any of the rules of the game. I could not stop the game. He was playing it according to the rules set out. We had no rule against what he was doing. He beat us at it. We have to let it play out the way it plays out and see what happens.
<v Darlene Lieblich Tipton>他没有违反游戏的任何规则。我不能停止游戏。他是按照设定的规则在玩。我们没有针对他所做的事情的规则。他在这一点上打败了我们。我们必须让它按它自己的方式进行，看看会发生什么。

00:47:46.320 --> 00:47:47.300
<v Michael Larson>Stop.
<v Michael Larson>停。

00:47:47.300 --> 00:47:49.740
<v Game Show Host>$5,000 and a spin.
<v Game Show Host>5000美元和一个旋转。

00:47:49.740 --> 00:48:02.560
<v Ira Glass>Michael's streak had gone so long that he busted the show's half hour format. The host did an impromptu interview to add time so the episode could be stretched into an hour-long special. (SUBJECT) GAME SHOW HOST: What are you going to do with the money, Michael?
<v Ira Glass>Michael的连胜持续太久了，以至于打破了节目的半小时格式。主持人进行了一次即兴采访以增加时间，这样这一集可以被拉长成一小时的特别节目。（游戏节目主持人：你打算怎么用这笔钱，Michael？）

00:48:02.560 --> 00:48:03.590
<v Michael Larson>Invest in houses.
<v Michael Larson>投资房子。

00:48:03.590 --> 00:48:04.600
<v Game Show Host>Real estate.
<v Game Show Host>房地产。

00:48:04.600 --> 00:48:05.550
<v Michael Larson>Yes, real estate.
<v Michael Larson>是的，房地产。

00:48:05.550 --> 00:48:07.410
<v Game Show Host>And not drive an ice cream truck.
<v Game Show Host>而且不用开冰淇淋车。

00:48:07.410 --> 00:48:09.000
<v Michael Larson>That's right.
<v Michael Larson>没错。

00:48:09.000 --> 00:48:24.190
<v Ira Glass>By the end of the show, Michael Larson had won a total of $110,237. Michael had shattered records that day, and not just for Press Your Luck, but for any American game show. He'd won more than anyone ever had in a single episode.
<v Ira Glass>节目结束时，Michael Larson总共赢了110,237美元。Michael那天打破了纪录，不仅仅是《Press Your Luck》，而是任何美国游戏节目的纪录。他在单集节目中赢的钱比任何人都要多。

00:48:24.190 --> 00:48:32.550
<v Game Show Host>Congratulations. Spend it wisely. And get your daughter a lovely birthday gift.
<v Game Show Host>恭喜。明智地花掉它。给你女儿买一份可爱的生日礼物。

00:48:32.550 --> 00:48:41.870
<v James Larson>I was happy for him. But I was worried that he might squander the money in some way.
<v James Larson>我为他感到高兴。但我担心他可能会以某种方式挥霍掉这笔钱。

00:48:41.870 --> 00:48:43.830
<v Ira Glass>Again, Michael's brother, James.
<v Ira Glass>再说一次，Michael的弟弟James。

00:48:43.830 --> 00:49:17.610
<v James Larson>Well yeah, I tried to get him to look at some reasonable investments, maybe buy some real estate. And he just put it into the bank, which is, I thought, a very wise move. Then after a couple of months, he took some of it out. I said, "What are you doing"? He says, "I'm trying to win this contest that they have on a radio station in Dayton. And you have to get a certain serial number".
<v James Larson>嗯是的，我试图让他看一些合理的投资，也许买些房地产。但他只是把钱存进银行，我认为这是一种非常明智的做法。几个月后，他取出了一些。我说，“你在干什么？”他说，“我在尝试赢取他们广播电台里举行的竞赛。你必须得到一个特定的序列号。”

00:49:17.610 --> 00:49:29.660
<v Teresa Bertram>Every day they would give a serial number out on the radio. And if you can match that serial number on a $1 dollar bill, then you won $30,000.
<v Teresa Bertram>他们每天会在收音机里给出一个序列号。如果你能在那张1美元纸币上匹配到那个序列号，那你就能赢30,000美元。

00:49:29.660 --> 00:49:32.180
<v Ira Glass>Again, Teresa, Michael's former wife.
<v Ira Glass>再说一次，Teresa，Michael的前妻。

00:49:32.180 --> 00:50:26.330
<v Teresa Bertram>That intrigued him to, "Well, I should be able to do. I got more $1 bills than anybody else around here". So it took him over two weeks. And it took him five different banks to get $100,000 and bring it in. And he was bringing it in like every other day in a burlap bag. And he even carried it in in a green trash bag. The federal reserve puts $1 bills in $4,000 dollar packets on a piece of little, thin balsam wood, and it's shrink-wrapped. We had like six of those under the bed. You walk into our bedroom and you could see them all lined up under the bed.
<v Teresa Bertram>这也吸引了他，“好吧，我应该能做到。我这里的1美元纸币比谁都多”。所以他花了两周多时间。他去了五家不同的银行取了100,000美元带进去。他每隔一天左右就用麻袋带进去。他甚至用绿色垃圾袋装着。美联储把1美元纸币存放在4,000美元一包的包装里，放在一块薄薄的轻木上，并用塑料薄膜包裹。床下我们大概有六个那样的包。你走进我们的卧室，就能看到它们整齐地排在床底下。

00:50:26.330 --> 00:50:37.490
<v Ira Glass>They also stacked hundreds of dollar bills on TVs, VCRs, the stairs, and just about everywhere else. After they had the 100 grand, they closed the shades and they hunkered down.
<v Ira Glass>他们还在电视、录像机、楼梯和几乎所有地方堆放了几百张纸币。在他们有了10万现金后，他们拉上窗帘，躲在那里。

00:50:37.490 --> 00:50:57.960
<v Teresa Bertram>We would sit and go through this money looking for these serial numbers. You had a few days to turn it in. So we had several numbers we were looking for. It took us a weeks to go through half that money, literally go through it. Because you have to eat, and bathe, and do other things.
<v Teresa Bertram>我们会坐下来翻阅这些钱寻找序列号。你有几天时间可以提交。所以我们有几个要寻找的号码。我们花了几周时间才翻完一半的钱，字面上是翻阅。因为你得吃饭、洗澡、做其他事情。

00:50:57.960 --> 00:51:18.880
<v Ira Glass>Day after day Michael and Teresa sorted the money into grocery bags and piles. They weren't finding the numbers the match the ones announced on the radio. So Michael put half the cash back in the bank. That left 50,000 $1 bills sitting in trash bags, hanging out of drawers, and falling out of closets. They were exhausted. So one night they took a break.
<v Ira Glass>日复一日，Michael和Teresa把钱整理成杂货袋和堆。他们没找到和收音机里宣布的一样的号码。所以Michael把一半现金存回了银行。剩下50,000张1美元纸币堆在垃圾袋里，挂在抽屉外面，从壁橱里掉出来。他们筋疲力尽。所以一天晚上，他们休息了一下。

00:51:18.880 --> 00:51:48.320
<v Teresa Bertram>We went to a Christmas party about 8 o'clock that evening, 7:30, 8 o'clock. And we were there for a long time. When we got home, it was near 1 o'clock or something. The back door was kicked in and his cash is gone.
<v Teresa Bertram>那天晚上8点，7:30，8点左右，我们去了个圣诞派对。我们在那里待了很长时间。当我们回家时，大概快1点了。后门被踢开了，他的现金没了。

00:51:48.320 --> 00:52:00.580
<v Teresa Bertram>The first thing he asked me was did I take his money. I said, "No, I didn't take your money. I was with you". "Well you had somebody do it." "No, I didn't have anybody do it".
<v Teresa Bertram>他问我的第一件事就是我是不是拿了他的钱。我说，“不，我没拿你的钱。我一直和你在一起”。“那你叫别人拿的。”“不，我没叫别人拿。”

00:52:00.580 --> 00:52:13.780
<v Ira Glass>To this day, this robbery has never been solved. But at the time, Michael was convinced Teresa had something to do with it. The police questioned Teresa several times. They turned up nothing and gave up. Michael didn't.
<v Ira Glass>直到今天，这起抢劫案仍未破获。但在当时，Michael确信Teresa与此事有关。警察审问了Teresa好几次。他们一无所获，放弃了。Michael没有放弃。

00:52:13.780 --> 00:52:36.130
<v Teresa Bertram>He kept watching me real close at night. And I mean he would come in and just stare at me and stuff. And I just had a feeling he was going to try to kill me. Yeah. He was going to put a gun to my head. At night while I was asleep, he'd come in and stand and just stare at me. And it gave me the creeps really bad.
<v Teresa Bertram>他晚上一直盯着我看得很紧。我是说他会进来，只是盯着我看什么的。我只是有一种感觉他想杀我。是的。他要用枪指着我的头。晚上我睡着的时候，他会进来站在那里盯着我看。这真的让我感到非常毛骨悚然。

00:52:36.130 --> 00:52:50.960
<v Teresa Bertram>So one day I just decided that while he ran to the store, and that's when I got my kids, and left, and went to a hotel, ordered a couple of pieces, and we just stayed there. And I called him and told him to get out of my house.
<v Teresa Bertram>所以有一天我决定，趁他去商店的时候，我带着孩子离开了，去了酒店，订了几间房，我们就待在那里。我打电话给他，叫他滚出我的房子。

00:52:50.960 --> 00:53:10.090
<v Ira Glass>After this point, Teresa and James say their picture of what Michael was up to is sketchy. Here's what we do know. Michael moved back to his little hometown outside Dayton, Ohio and he found a new girlfriend. In 1999, nine years after Michael's big win, Michael and this girlfriend suddenly fled Ohio.
<v Ira Glass>从这一点开始，Teresa和James说他们对Michael正在做的事情的了解就很模糊了。我们确实知道的是，Michael搬回了俄亥俄州代顿外的小家乡，并找了一个新女友。1999年，在Michael大获全胜九年后，Michael和这个女友突然逃离了俄亥俄州。

00:53:10.090 --> 00:53:39.240
<v Ira Glass>Teresa and James later learned why from federal investigators. The pair was caught up in a huge scam that combined a pyramid scheme, fake investments, and Indian lotteries. Altogether 20,000 people were conned out of $3 million. It was also the first major internet fraud that the Securities and Exchange Commission ever investigated. The SEC and the FBI hundred Michael and this woman for almost four years until 1999 when Michael died in Florida from throat cancer.
<v Ira Glass>Teresa和James后来从联邦调查员那里得知了原因。两人陷入了一个巨大的骗局，结合了金字塔计划、虚假投资和印度彩票。总共20,000人被骗走了300万美元。这也是美国证券交易委员会调查的第一起重大互联网欺诈案。SEC和FBI追踪了Michael和这个女人近四年，直到1999年Michael在佛罗里达州死于喉癌。

00:53:39.240 --> 00:54:05.900
<v Ira Glass>Teresa says even today she has bad feelings about how Michael treated her. After all, he accused her of taking his money. And when Michael ran away from the Feds, he left behind a son he'd had with Teresa. Because of that, it's easy to get her to say bad things about him, about the kind of person he was. That makes this one thing she said kind of surprising, which is that it actually bothers her when sometimes people say he cheated Press Your Luck out of money. They call what he did a scandal or a scam.
<v Ira Glass>Teresa说即使今天，她对Michael对待她的方式仍有不好的感觉。毕竟，他指控她拿了他的钱。当Michael从联邦调查员那里逃跑时，他留下了一个他和Teresa生的儿子。正因为如此，很容易让她说他的坏话，说他是什么样的人。这使得她说的这件事有点令人惊讶，那就是当人们说他从《Press Your Luck》骗钱时，这其实让她感到困扰。他们称他的行为是丑闻或骗局。

00:54:05.900 --> 00:54:20.950
<v Teresa Bertram>I will say the game show is one thing he did do that was honest. He didn't cheat. There's no cheating to memorizing something. He'd sit here for six months and studied it. That was pretty amazing.
<v Teresa Bertram>我想说这个游戏节目是他做过的一件诚实的事。他没有作弊。记住某些东西并不叫作弊。他在那里坐了六个月并研究它。这真的非常了不起。

00:54:20.950 --> 00:54:28.040
<v Ira Glass>But Michael's brother, James, still has mixed feelings about Michael's performance on Press Your Luck more than 25 years later.
<v Ira Glass>但Michael的弟弟James在25年后对Michael在《Press Your Luck》上的表现仍有复杂的情绪。

00:54:28.040 --> 00:54:55.010
<v James Larson>Well I fell that by winning the game show, that was the start of his downfall. Him winning that large amount of money convinced him in his own mind that he could trick anybody, that he could do just about anything.
<v James Larson>嗯，我感到赢得那个游戏节目是他堕落的开始。赢得那么大一笔钱让他自己在心里确信他可以戏耍任何人，他几乎可以做任何事。

00:54:55.010 --> 00:55:14.840
<v Ira Glass>I ask him, if there was some way for him to go back and change what happen, maybe rig the game so Michael would have lost on Press Your Luck, would he do it? James says no. He's glad Michael won. He just wishes his brother had been a different person, the kind of person who could just be happy with what he's got.
<v Ira Glass>我问他，如果有什么方法能让他回去改变发生的事情，也许操纵游戏让Michael在《Press Your Luck》输掉，他会做吗？James说不会。他很高兴Michael赢了。他只是希望他弟弟能成为一个不同的人，那种能满足于所拥有的一切的人。

00:55:14.840 --> 00:55:21.300
<v Game Show Host>Michael, when you had $30,000, $40,000, $50,000, what was in your mind? Why did you keep going?
<v Game Show Host>Michael，当你有了30,000，40,000，50,000美元时，你在想什么？为什么你还要继续？

00:55:21.300 --> 00:55:39.260
<v Michael Larson>Well two things, one, it felt right. And the second was I still had seven spins, and if I passed them--
<v Michael Larson>嗯两件事，第一，感觉是对的。第二是我还有七次旋转，如果我跳过它们——

00:55:39.260 --> 00:55:40.500
<v Ira Glass>Shawn Allee in Chicago.
<v Ira Glass>Shawn Allee在芝加哥。

